// The QUIET half of RV2-DEBT-203, on its own so nothing can mask it.
//
// Its sibling `arrays_fixed_across_calls.sg` also annotates types, and an
// annotation fails LOUDLY when signature keys lose a fixed array's length —
// `cannot assign [Item] to [Item; 2]`, at compile time, before anything runs.
// That masks the failure that actually mattered: with the length dropped, the
// INFERRED spelling compiled and the two sides of the call disagreed about the
// ABI. The callee returned through an sret pointer into inline storage while
// the caller had allocated a single word to receive it, and the sixteen bytes
// were then read back as an array handle and freed as a heap buffer.
//
// So this file annotates nothing. Break the key producers and it does not
// refuse — it dies: the VM on a stale storage reference, and the native binary
// by exiting 255 with an empty stdout and an empty stderr, which is the worst
// shape this corpus can carry because a runner that only reads exit codes sees
// a number and no reason.

type Item = { id: int, num: int };

fn build() -> Item[2] {
    return [Item { id = 11, num = 1 }, Item { id = 22, num = 2 }];
}

// Nothing below states a fixed-array type. `build`'s own return annotation is
// the only one in the file, and it is unavoidable — a function has to say what
// it returns. Every USE is inferred, which is what keeps the failure quiet:
// with no second spelling to compare against, nothing refuses, and the call
// just lies about its ABI. Adding so much as one `let x: Item[2] = ...` here
// would turn this file into a compile-time refusal and hide the very shape it
// exists to catch.
@entrypoint
fn main() -> int {
    let direct = build();
    print((direct[0].id) to string);
    print((direct[1].id) to string);
    print((direct[1].num) to string);

    return 0;
}
